  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22829v1</id>
    <title>KKM meets colorful Carathéodory</title>
    <updated>2026-10-16T02:11:09Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22829v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.22829v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>The colorful Carathéodory theorem states that if $V_1,\dots,V_{d+1}\subset\mathbb R^d$ satisfy $0\in\mathrm{conv}\;V_i$ for every $i$, then there exist points $v_i\in V_i$ such that $0\in\mathrm{conv}\;\{v_1,\dots,v_{d+1}\}$.
  We give a new proof based on triangulations and the classical KKM lemma. We lift the sets $V_i$ to parallel affine copies of $\mathbb R^d$ in $\mathbb R^d\times\mathbb R^d$ and use a triangulation of the resulting point configuration to construct a KKM cover. Using the same approach, we prove the very colorful Carathéodory theorem.</summary>
    <category term="math.CO" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="math.HO" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
